      modelAnswer: 'There is strong evidence that the EU is a significant global actor, particularly in economic affairs. It is one of the largest economies in the world and the largest trading bloc, and it negotiates trade agreements on behalf of all its members, giving it considerable bargaining power. Through the size and importance of its single market, the EU is able to set global standards in areas such as data protection, product safety and competition law, a phenomenon described as the "Brussels effect", as companies worldwide adapt to EU rules to access its market. The EU\'s General Data Protection Regulation has influenced data protection laws around the world. Liberals argue that the EU\'s influence as a "normative power" rests on its ability to spread its values and rules through trade, aid and diplomacy.\n\nThe EU has also acted as a significant global actor in response to Russia\'s invasion of Ukraine. It has imposed successive packages of sanctions on Russia, provided substantial financial and military assistance to Ukraine, including a €50 billion Ukraine Facility agreed in 2024, and granted Ukraine candidate status. It has also taken a leading role on climate change, through ambitious emissions targets and its role in international climate negotiations, and it is one of the world\'s largest providers of development aid.\n\nHowever, the EU\'s significance as a global actor is limited in foreign and security policy. The Common Foreign and Security Policy depends on unanimity, which means that a single member state can block decisions, as Hungary has repeatedly done over sanctions and aid to Ukraine. The EU lacks its own military forces and depends on NATO, and ultimately on the USA, for its security. Realists argue that the EU is an economic giant but a political and military dwarf, since global power ultimately depends on hard power, which remains in the hands of individual member states. Member states continue to pursue their own foreign policies, and divisions between them, for example over relations with China and the conflict in the Middle East, weaken the EU\'s ability to speak with one voice.\n\nThe EU\'s global influence has also been challenged by internal difficulties. The eurozone crisis, the migration crisis of 2015 and Brexit, which removed one of its largest military and diplomatic powers, raised questions about its cohesion and capacity to act.\n\nOn balance, the EU has become a significant global actor in economic affairs, regulation and trade, where its collective power and the size of its market give it genuine global influence, and its response to the war in Ukraine has strengthened its role in foreign policy. But its significance in security and defence remains limited by its dependence on the USA, the requirement for unanimity and the continuing independence of its member states. The EU is therefore a significant global actor, but a distinctive one whose influence rests more on economic and normative power than on military strength.'
